  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe pattern over the next few days will be very typical of a\nstrong El Nino during the late Fall, characterized by\nunseasonably warm and dry conditions across the Great Lakes and\nOhio Valley. Infrared satellite and water vapor loops this \nafternoon show a parade of strong storm systems moving toward \nthe West Coast, leading to split mid/upper flow and an active \nsubtropical jet extension across Mexico and the Gulf Coast. This\noverall pattern is carrying mild Pacific-based air eastward\nacross the CONUS, with a broad mid/upper trough over the West\nand broad mid/upper ridge over the East. This will lock the\nsurface high, currently centered over the Appalachians and Mid\nAtlantic, in place through the end of the week. A weak low\npressure system and associated mid/upper shortwave lifting from\nthe northern Plains tonight through the northern Great Lakes\nThursday will briefly flatten the mid/upper ridge enough to push\na weakening cold front to the vicinity of Lake Erie Thursday\nnight, but this front will wash out as the ridge rebounds in\nresponse to a stronger storm system and associated trough\nprogressing from the western CONUS into the Plains Friday and\nSaturday. The end result will be continued southerly flow and \nwarm, dry conditions through Saturday, with highs in the upper\n50s/low 60s and lows generally in the mid 30s to low 40s. These\ntemperatures will average about 10 degrees above normal. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nBy Saturday night and Sunday, the aforementioned mid/upper\ntrough moving across the Plains will attempt to phase with a\nnorthern stream shortwave/closed low dropping out of Alberta.\nThis will support a modestly deep surface low lifting\nnortheastward through the Great Lakes Sunday, dragging a strong\ncold front through the region Sunday night. Guidance is pretty\nmuch in agreement with this evolution except with how the front\nmay interact with a subtropical low near the Carolinas.\nUncertainty exists pertaining to that, which could eventually\nlead to the front slowing. At this point, expect fairly\nwidespread showers along and ahead of the front late Saturday\nnight and Sunday. Temperatures will rapidly fall in the wake of\nthe front Sunday night and Monday as the mid/upper trough swings\nacross the Great Lakes, and the strong cold air advection and NW\nflow will lead to lake effect rain/snow showers over the primary\nand secondary snowbelts. Expect the lake effect to end quickly\nMonday night as strong Canadian high pressure builds across the\nGreat Lakes setting up dry but continued cold conditions through\nmid week.",
